WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.800
NLTK.

2
00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:05.309
The classic teaching library for language processing.

3
00:00:05.309 --> 00:00:08.033
It's everywhere in tutorials and legacy code.

4
00:00:09.233 --> 00:00:13.533
NLTK, the Natural Language Toolkit, dates from two thousand one.

5
00:00:13.833 --> 00:00:19.000
It's pure Python with an academic flavor, and it bundles corpora and classic tools.

6
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:23.333
It's slow and dated for production, so know it, but don't build on it.

7
00:00:24.533 --> 00:00:25.733
Here's the toolbox.

8
00:00:26.033 --> 00:00:30.800
Ready-made corpora like Gutenberg and Brown give you instant sandbox text.

9
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.533
Sentence and word splitters, the pre-subword tokenizers.

10
00:00:34.833 --> 00:00:39.233
Part of speech taggers that label each word as noun, verb, adjective.

11
00:00:39.533 --> 00:00:42.933
And stemmers, the WordNet lemmatizer, and synonym graphs.

12
00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:48.200
NLTK also gave us the classical pipeline vocabulary.

13
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:51.233
Tokenize the text into sentences and words.

14
00:00:51.533 --> 00:00:53.800
Tag each token with its part of speech.

15
00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.300
Then chunk tagged words into phrases.

16
00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:58.500
This sequence turns up in interviews.

17
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:00.900
Here it is in code.

18
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:02.840
First, we download the models.

19
00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:03.933
They aren't bundled.

20
00:01:04.233 --> 00:01:06.333
We split the paragraph into sentences.

21
00:01:06.633 --> 00:01:11.200
Then we tag every word and keep the ones whose tag starts with N N, the nouns.

22
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.367
The classic gotcha: you run a tokenizer on a fresh machine and get a lookup error.

23
00:01:17.667 --> 00:01:19.653
The data was never downloaded.

24
00:01:19.653 --> 00:01:22.433
Call nltk download for the resource first.

25
00:01:23.633 --> 00:01:25.500
Here's NLTK at its best.

26
00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:31.233
Before designing metadata for the RAG, you spend ten notebook minutes probing a sample of logs.

27
00:01:31.533 --> 00:01:36.000
Transcripts average nine words per sentence, engineering reports thirty one.

28
00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.349
That's evidence they need different chunk sizes.

29
00:01:39.349 --> 00:01:41.700
Quick, disposable corpus archaeology.

30
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:43.782
Let's recap.

31
00:01:43.782 --> 00:01:46.500
NLTK is the classic teaching library.

32
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.267
It bundles corpora, tokenizers, taggers, and stemmers.

33
00:01:50.567 --> 00:01:53.367
Remember to download resources on first use.

34
00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:56.194
It's great for probing, not production.

35
00:01:56.194 --> 00:01:57.167
Next up: spaCy.
